Q: How to handle button actions inside a SwiftUI List? A: <p>In addition to implement the action you have to change and keep the value in the <code>Post</code> instance, and due to value type you have to do it directly in the instance in the <code>feeds</code> array.</p>
<p>My suggestion is to add a handler in <code>FeedRow</code> and run the action in <code>FeedListView</code>, because there the <code>index</code> is available. The action sets <code>isLiked</code> to <code>true</code> if it's <code>false</code> and increments <code>likersCount</code></p>
<pre><code>struct Post {
    var title: String
    var isLiked = false
    var likersCount = 0
}

@Observable class FeedListViewModel {
    var feeds = [Post]()

    init() {
        feeds = [Post(title: &quot;Post 1&quot;), Post(title: &quot;Post 2&quot;), Post(title: &quot;Post 3&quot;)]
    }
}

struct FeedListView: View {

    @State private var viewModel = FeedListViewModel()

    var body: some View {
        ForEach(0..&lt;viewModel.feeds.count, id: \.self) { index in
            FeedRow(post: viewModel.feeds[index]) {
                if !viewModel.feeds[index].isLiked {
                    viewModel.feeds[index].isLiked = true
                }
                viewModel.feeds[index].likersCount += 1
            }
        }
    }
}

struct FeedRow: View {
    let post: Post
    let action : () -&gt; Void

    var body: some View {
        HStack {
            Text(post.title)
            Button(action: {
                action()
            }) {
                Image(systemName: post.isLiked ? &quot;hand.raised.fill&quot; : &quot;hand.raised&quot;)
            }
            if post.likersCount &gt; 0 {
                Text(String(post.likersCount))
            }
        }
    }
}


</code></pre>

Q: Insert variables in a do loop A: <p>I think you mean can you use a variable list in that form?  The answer is no.</p>
<p>You can use a colon to make a variable list of variables whose names start with the same string.  So a statement like:</p>
<pre class="lang-none prettyprint-override"><code>array t type: ;
</code></pre>
<p>Would find variables like TYPE1_1 and TYPE2_3.  There would appear in the list in the order they were defined to the data step (say from reading an existing dataset that contained them).</p>
<p>If that does not work then you will need to use some code generation to generate the list of variable names in the order you need.</p>
<p>For example,  if you enclose your code in a macro definition then you could use a %DO loop to generate code that references the variables in the order you request.</p>
<pre class="lang-none prettyprint-override"><code>array t 
%do i=1 to &amp;n ;
  test&amp;i.__2
%end;
;
</code></pre>
<p>If the variables already exist in a dataset then you query the metadata for that dataset and select the names from there into a macro variable that you could later use to place the list of variable names into your code.</p>
<pre class="lang-none prettyprint-override"><code>proc sql noprint;
select name into :varlist
from dictionary.columns
where libname='WORK' and memname='HAVE'
  and upcase(name) like 'TEST%^_^_2' escape '^'
  and 1 &lt;= input(scan(substr(name,5),1,'_'),?32.) &lt;= &amp;n
;
quit;
...
array t &amp;varlist;
...
</code></pre>

Q: Query deeply nested document with dynamic key A: <p>You <em>can</em> do this using a <code>find</code> query with a <a href="https://www.mongodb.com/docs/manual/tutorial/project-fields-from-query-results/" rel="nofollow noreferrer">projection</a> but it's harder to debug and a bit ugly. (I recommend using an aggregation pipeline like in <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/a/79885071/1431750">Wernfried Domscheit's answer</a>.)</p>
<ol>
<li>Use a regular <code>find</code> with the sub-objects' fields</li>
<li>Then a projection with nested <code>$map</code>'s &amp; <code>$filter</code>'s to get just the matching sub arrays for the card and user. Which are just aggregation stages being used in the <code>project</code> part of <code>find</code>.</li>
</ol>
<p>The result is a nested list and if you expect at most one match per document, then combine those with an <code>arrayElemAt</code> with <code>0</code> to only get the first one.</p>
<pre><code>db.collection.find({
  _id: &quot;693ae6dc320b2bb92635361e&quot;,
  &quot;cards._id&quot;: &quot;600056266&quot;,
  &quot;cards.users._id&quot;: &quot;EafXiJktdKSM45OcnniU4gpcGWc2&quot;
},
{
  // all this is in the `project` part of the find query
  _id: 0,
  votes: {
    $map: {
      input: {
        $filter: {
          input: &quot;$cards&quot;,
          as: &quot;c&quot;,
          cond: { $eq: [&quot;$$c._id&quot;, &quot;600056266&quot;] }
        }
      },
      as: &quot;resultCards&quot;,
      in: {
        $map: {
          input: {
            $filter: {
              input: &quot;$$resultCards.users&quot;,
              as: &quot;u&quot;,
              cond: { $eq: [&quot;$$u._id&quot;, &quot;EafXiJktdKSM45OcnniU4gpcGWc2&quot; ] }
            }
          },
          as: &quot;user&quot;,
          in: &quot;$$user.vote&quot;
        }
      }
    }
  }
})
</code></pre>
